  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Small-ensemble wedding and dance music of the Ng'ambo neighbourhoods of Zanzibar Town, long described as the popular, participatory counterpart of orchestral taarab and sharing much of its song repertoire. A singer and chorus are backed by one or two violins, the sanduku (a one-string tea-chest bass), a pair of small clay drums that give the genre its name, and rattles; the hand-drum card stands in for the kidumbak drums and the shaker card for the rattles.",
  "recipe": "classic modern kidumbak, groove Percussion, central east south African branch. live take. fiddle, tea chest bass, hand drum source shaker source unspecified. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
